Service state|systemctl status APP.service --no-pager|Summarizes a systemd service's state and recent messages.|Check whether startup failed, exited or remains active.|Loaded describes the unit definition; Active describes current state; Main PID identifies the process.|Active: failed (Result: exit-code)|Replace APP with the actual unit; read-only inspection.
Effective unit|systemctl cat APP.service|Prints the unit file and drop-in configuration.|Find overrides or unexpected startup arguments.|File headers identify each source; later applicable overrides modify the base definition.|# /etc/systemd/system/APP.service.d/override.conf|Read-only; displayed configuration may contain sensitive environment values.
Service journal|journalctl -u APP.service --since '-30 min' --no-pager|Filters journal entries by service and recent time.|Correlate startup failures with a change.|Read timestamps, process identifiers and errors together; retention can remove older evidence.|APP[123]: configuration error at line ...|Read-only; redact secrets before sharing logs.
Boot failures|journalctl -b -p err --no-pager|Shows error-priority messages from the current boot.|Narrow boot and initialization problems.|-b selects the boot and -p err includes error and more severe priorities; warnings are excluded.|timestamp host unit: error message|Read-only; inspect a broader priority range if necessary.
Failed units|systemctl --failed --no-pager|Lists units currently in failed state.|Find failed dependencies after reboot or maintenance.|UNIT names the failed object; ACTIVE and SUB describe its state.|UNIT LOAD ACTIVE SUB DESCRIPTION|Read-only; a previously recovered failure may no longer appear.
Timer schedule|systemctl list-timers --all --no-pager|Lists systemd timers and their recent and next triggers.|Diagnose jobs that run late or not at all.|NEXT and LEFT show expected scheduling; LAST and PASSED show prior activation; ACTIVATES names the target unit.|NEXT LEFT LAST PASSED UNIT ACTIVATES|Read-only; inspect the activated service's logs to verify job success.
Clock status|timedatectl status|Displays system clock, timezone and synchronization status.|Check clock assumptions during TLS or log-order problems.|System clock synchronized and NTP service are different indicators; inspect the time source for detailed offset.|System clock synchronized: yes|Read-only; command availability depends on the host's service stack.
Storage layers|lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS|Lists block-device hierarchy and selected properties.|Distinguish disk, partition and filesystem sizes before expansion.|Indented children belong to parent devices; a filesystem can be smaller than the underlying block device.|sda ... / ; sdb ... /data|Read-only; MOUNTPOINTS support depends on util-linux version.
Mount validation|findmnt --verify --verbose|Checks filesystem-table parsing and usability assumptions.|Find fstab problems before a reboot.|Warnings and errors identify entries requiring investigation; success does not simulate every boot dependency.|Success, no errors or warnings detected|Read-only verification; visibility and device availability affect results.
SSH configuration check|sshd -t|Validates sshd configuration and host-key sanity.|Catch syntax errors before reloading remote access.|No output and zero exit status normally indicate validation success; errors name the problematic setting.|No output on success|May require elevated read access; validation alone does not prove a new login will succeed.
Log rotation preview|logrotate --debug /etc/logrotate.conf|Parses rotation policy and explains planned decisions without rotating logs.|Diagnose wrong paths, retention and rotation timing.|Look for the selected log, last rotation and whether rotation is due.|considering log /var/log/app.log|Debug mode does not update state or rotate files; output may include filesystem details.
Sudo syntax check|visudo -c|Checks installed sudoers policy syntax.|Verify privilege-policy integrity before ending an administrative session.|Parsed OK identifies accepted files; line errors identify syntax faults.|/etc/sudoers: parsed OK|Read-only check, usually requires privileged access; keep a working recovery session during policy edits.
